the successful societies program was  established ten years ago and has  brought together a highly  interdisciplinary group of people to  have a conversation together about how  to make societies better places one of  the strengths of our group now is that  we've worked together for 10 years  and i think we're we've developed a  remarkable capacity to understand or to  to function a little bit like a  multi-headed hydra  it's quite unusual for social scientists  from as many different  specialties  as are represented in this book or in  the program to get together and have  sustained conversations we have you know  we have historians political scientists  philosophers  economists  people in public health etc etc  psychology so one of the first things  that our project did that became this  book was to try and make a clearer um  understanding and  a better uh classification of the  elements of neoliberalism and so our our  task was first of all to get a clearer  assessment of what actually changed  uh and what didn't change what what were  the continuities as well as the changes  and then to and then to explore what  were some of the effects of those  changes it may lead people to look at  neoliberalism in a different way  and  i would say in a more optimistic way  there's a number of other  values that drives human being besides  profit maximization and this is the kind  of questions that i think we're  particularly well equipped to address  well we hope that  many  scholars and students and members of the  general public will find in this book  a better way of understanding some of  the more most basic features of their  lives  as as those lives are lived in the midst  of  neoliberal reforms neoliberal ideas what  makes it possible for  a community or even a nation  to absorb some kind of environmental or  economic shock and then come out the  other side stronger than they went in  you
 

